# LAB 01 - Install and Verify Kubernetes Environment

## 🎯 Objective

In this lab, you will learn how to:

- Understand what Kubernetes is
- Understand a Kubernetes Cluster
- Understand the Control Plane and Worker Node
- Install and verify Minikube
- Install and verify kubectl
- Start a Kubernetes Cluster
- Verify cluster health
- View cluster information
- Explore Kubernetes namespaces
- View system Pods

---

# 📖 What is Kubernetes?

Kubernetes (also called **K8s**) is an open-source container orchestration platform that automates the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications.

Imagine running hundreds of containers manually. If one crashes, you would have to restart it yourself. Kubernetes monitors your applications and automatically recreates failed containers, scales applications when needed, and distributes workloads across available nodes.

In simple words:

> Kubernetes manages containers so your applications stay available and healthy.

---

# 📖 What is a Kubernetes Cluster?

A Kubernetes Cluster is a group of machines working together to run containerized applications.

A cluster consists of:

- Control Plane
- One or more Worker Nodes

---

# 📖 Kubernetes Architecture

```text
                 Kubernetes Cluster
+------------------------------------------------+
|                                                |
|             Control Plane                      |
|----------------------------------------------- |
| API Server                                     |
| Scheduler                                      |
| Controller Manager                             |
| etcd                                           |
|                                                |
|                manages cluster                 |
|                      │                         |
|                      ▼                         |
|               Worker Node                      |
|----------------------------------------------- |
| kubelet                                       |
| kube-proxy                                    |
| Pods                                          |
| Containers                                    |
|                                                |
+------------------------------------------------+
```

---

# 📖 What is the Control Plane?

The Control Plane is the brain of Kubernetes.

It is responsible for:

- Managing the cluster
- Scheduling Pods
- Monitoring the cluster
- Maintaining the desired state
- Responding to failures

Without the Control Plane, Kubernetes cannot function.

---

# 📖 What is a Worker Node?

A Worker Node is where your applications actually run.

Each Worker Node contains:

- kubelet
- kube-proxy
- Pods
- Containers

---

# 📖 What is Minikube?

Minikube creates a local Kubernetes cluster on your laptop.

Instead of creating multiple servers in AWS or another cloud provider, Minikube allows you to learn Kubernetes on a single machine.

---

# 📖 What is kubectl?

`kubectl` is the command-line tool used to communicate with the Kubernetes API Server.

It allows you to:

- Create resources
- Delete resources
- View cluster information
- Troubleshoot applications
- Manage Kubernetes

---

# 🛠️ Prerequisites

Make sure the following software is installed:

- Docker Desktop
- Minikube
- kubectl
- Git
- Visual Studio Code

---

# Step 1 - Verify Docker Installation

```bash
docker version
```

Expected Output

```text
Client:
 Version: xx.xx.x

Server:
 Version: xx.xx.x
```

Docker must be running before starting Minikube.

---

# Step 2 - Verify Minikube Installation

```bash
minikube version
```

Expected Output

```text
minikube version: v1.xx.x
```

---

# Step 3 - Verify kubectl Installation

```bash
kubectl version --client
```

Expected Output

```text
Client Version: v1.xx.x
```

---

# Step 4 - Start Minikube

```bash
minikube start
```

Expected Output

```text
Done! kubectl is now configured to use "minikube" cluster.
```

The first startup may take several minutes.

---

# Step 5 - Verify Cluster Status

```bash
minikube status
```

Expected Output

```text
host: Running
kubelet: Running
apiserver: Running
kubeconfig: Configured
```

---

# Step 6 - View Cluster Nodes

```bash
kubectl get nodes
```

Expected Output

```text
NAME       STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION
minikube   Ready    control-plane   xxm   v1.xx.x
```

### Understanding the Output

| Column | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| NAME | Name of the node |
| STATUS | Current node status |
| ROLES | Control Plane or Worker |
| AGE | How long the node has existed |
| VERSION | Kubernetes version |

---

# Step 7 - View Cluster Information

```bash
kubectl cluster-info
```

Expected Output

```text
Kubernetes control plane is running at https://127.0.0.1:xxxxx

CoreDNS is running at ...
```

---

# Step 8 - View Namespaces

```bash
kubectl get namespaces
```

Expected Output

```text
NAME              STATUS   AGE
default           Active
kube-system       Active
kube-public       Active
kube-node-lease   Active
```

### Understanding Namespaces

Namespaces help organize resources inside a Kubernetes cluster.

By default, all applications are created in the **default** namespace unless another namespace is specified.

---

# Step 9 - View System Pods

```bash
kubectl get pods -A
```

Expected Output

You should see Kubernetes system Pods such as:

- coredns
- etcd
- kube-apiserver
- kube-controller-manager
- kube-scheduler

These Pods are responsible for running and managing the Kubernetes cluster.

---

# Step 10 - Stop the Cluster (Optional)

```bash
minikube stop
```

---

# Step 11 - Start the Cluster Again

```bash
minikube start
```

This confirms that your cluster can be stopped and restarted successfully.

---

# 🐞 Common Errors and Solutions

## Error

```text
'minikube' is not recognized as an internal or external command
```

### Solution

Verify that Minikube is installed correctly and added to your system PATH.

---

## Error

```text
Docker is not running
```

### Solution

Start Docker Desktop and wait until it shows that the Docker Engine is running.

---

## Error

```text
Unable to connect to the server
```

### Solution

Verify the cluster status.

```bash
minikube status
```

If the cluster is stopped, start it again.

```bash
minikube start
```

